<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">French Saint Croix</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 CE – 1733 CE · capital Bassin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">French colony on Saint Croix, briefly owned by the Knights of Malta, abandoned in the 1690s and finally sold to Denmark in 1733.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a> had been contested by English, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Spanish settlers. In 1650 forces sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</a>, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-antilles">French Antilles</a>, expelled the Spanish and occupied the island. The next year it was included in the sale of French islands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, who held it until 1665.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony centred on a small settlement at Bassin, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiansted">Christiansted</a>. Settlers grew tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> with enslaved labour, but disease and the humid climate took a heavy toll. After 1665 it was run by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> and then the crown, as a minor dependency of the larger French islands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1695 and 1696 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> withdrew the settlers to strengthen <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, leaving the island largely empty for decades. France sold it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-west-india-and-guinea-company">Danish West India and Guinea Company</a> in 1733, after which it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> centre of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/danish-west-indies">Danish West Indies</a>, later part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-virgin-islands">United States Virgin Islands</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1650 CE – 1733 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.54%;width:1.65%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 CE – 1733 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bassin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states-virgin-islands">United States Virgin Islands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sainte-Croix</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1692&amp;focus=french-saint-croix" aria-label="French Saint Croix on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2922 1519 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Saint Croix lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M3295 1791h0M3240 1753h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3242 1775h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3242 1775h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United States Virgin Islands. <a href="/atlas?year=1692&amp;focus=french-saint-croix">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
